<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as magus, magoi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The magi (singular magus) were priests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and earlier Iranian religions. The earliest known use of the word is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, of about 520 BCE, and in the mid-5th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described the magi both as a tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and as a priestly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> who interpreted omens and dreams. Through Greek the word came to mean a practitioner of magic, the source of the modern words magic and magician. In the Gospel of Matthew, magi from the east pay homage to the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, a use rendered in English as kings or wise men. A hereditary Zoroastrian priesthood survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magi.jpg" alt="Magi" width="330" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nickmard Khoey · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_statuettes_from_the_Oxus_Treasure_by_Nickmard_Khoey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word appears only twice in Iranian texts from before the 5th century BCE, and only one of those can be dated precisely. It occurs in the trilingual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, of about 520 BCE, where certain rebels are called magian; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> portion the word is maγu-, generally assumed to be a loanword from Median, and its meaning there is uncertain.</p><p>The other instance, in the Younger Avesta, is the word moghu.tbiš, meaning hostile to the moghu, where moghu seems to mean a member of the tribe or a social class rather than a magus. A common Iranian term for a member of the tribe may have taken, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, the sense of a member of the priestly tribe, and so a priest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the mid-5th century BCE Herodotus used the word in two senses: for one of the tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, and for a sacerdotal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> whose ethnic origin is never mentioned again. He also showed the magi as interpreters of omens and dreams. In the early 4th century BCE Xenophon, who had seen the Persian court, depicted them as authorities on all religious matters and as responsible for educating the future king. Magi are recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, Media and elsewhere, and in non-Iranian lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Greek mageia soon came to mean something close to magic in the modern sense, often with a pejorative tone, and magos came to mean a conjurer or charlatan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> made Pseudo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> the founder of the magi and the inventor of astrology and magic, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, in the first century, named Zoroaster as the inventor of magic and called it a monstrous craft. The word magos displaced the older Greek goēs for a practitioner of magic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the Gospel of Matthew, magi from the east, guided by a star, pay homage to the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and present gold, frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>; the plural magi entered English from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in this context around 1200, and the word is often rendered as kings or wise men. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> depicts the magi as sorcerers who obstructed Jewish practice.</p><p>In Arabic, majus is the term for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>, and it occurs in the Qur'an, in sura 22 verse 17. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the Sakaldwipiya Brahmins are held to descend from Maga priests invited to worship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>. A hereditary Zoroastrian priesthood survives in India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with ranks called Herbad, Mobad and Dastur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Magi</b> who bring gifts to the infant Jesus in the Gospel of Matthew bear the title of the priests of Iran, the magi, who were the guardians of the Zoroastrian tradition.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Book II (Euterpe) turns to the geography, customs and kings of Egypt, and Book III (Thalia) to Cambyses, the conspiracy against the <b>Magi</b>, the rise of Darius I and the twenty satrapies.</blockquote><a href="/work/histories-of-herodotus">Histories of Herodotus · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>magi</b> were the priests of the Persians and Medes.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The three ships are thought to go back to those that carried the purported relics of the Biblical <b>Magi</b> to Cologne Cathedral in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/i-saw-three-ships">I Saw Three Ships</a></li><li><blockquote>The verses tell of his birth into the world, of the three <b>Magi</b> who came with gifts, and end by calling on clergy and choirboys alike to sing like the angels, with glory to God in the highest.</blockquote><a href="/songs/personent-hodie">Personent hodie</a></li><li><blockquote>…announcement to the shepherds and the shepherds' adoration are told in the second chapter of the Gospel of Luke; the Star of Bethlehem belongs to the story of the <b>Magi</b>, the Wise Men, in the Gospel of Matthew, and does not appear in the story of the shepherds.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-first-nowell">The First Nowell</a></li></ul></section>
